## Founding & History
The vines at what is now Swinging Gate Vineyard were originally planted in 1985 and were known as Buchanan Vineyard. For approximately 15 years, the fruit from these vines was sold to various Tasmanian wineries but the vineyard did not have its own identity or label. The property was subsequently sold and remained abandoned for a period. In 2014, horticulturalist Doug Cox and his wife Corrie purchased the vineyard and began its rejuvenation. The name "Swinging Gate" refers to the gates of the vineyard swinging open again after many years. The first batch of estate-grown wine was produced in 2015, marking the 30th vintage of the established vines. The old machinery shed on the property was transformed into the cellar door.

## People
Swinging Gate Vineyard is run by the Cox Family, with Doug and Corrie Cox as the current owners. Doug Cox, who had a 30-year career in horticulture, taught himself the winemaking process after purchasing the vineyard. A notable member of the team is the wine dog, Nellie, who is described as a "cover girl" and "celebrity wine dog".

## Vineyard & Site
The vineyard is situated in the premium area of the Tamar Valley, positioned just above the mist, which contributes to cool nights and a sunny aspect, creating ideal environmental conditions for producing premium wine. The property spans 17.2 hectares (approximately 42.5 acres), with 9.3 hectares (approximately 23 acres) under vine. The slopes are predominantly north-east facing. The original vines were planted in 1985.

Varietals planted include Chardonnay, Frontignac, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris, Pinot Noir, Cabernet Sauvignon, and Merlot. The vineyard also produces grapes for Sparkling, Sparkling Rosé, Riesling, Rosé, and other white and red wines, including Pet Nat's. The farming approach involves minimal intervention and significant manual effort. The vineyard is tended in a holistic and sustainable manner, with fruit being hand-picked, crushed, and fermented using traditional methods. Doug Cox employs a light touch in managing the old vines. Yields typically range between 6 to 8 tonnes per hectare, subject to seasonal variations. Among the Pinot Noir clones planted are sub-clones of the Pommard clone D4V2, known as "Smart Clones," covering about 1 hectare.

## Cellar Door Experience
The cellar door operates Monday to Saturday from 10 am to 5 pm, and on Sundays from 12 pm to 5 pm. It is closed on Christmas Day, New Year's Day, Boxing Day, and Good Friday. While walk-ins are generally welcome, bookings are highly recommended for groups of four or more, especially during holiday periods. Online bookings are available for groups of up to eight guests.

Visitors can enjoy guided tastings with a rotating selection of wines grown and processed on-site. Tasting options include up to five wines from a selected list for $12 or eight wines for $17. The cellar door offers an informal tasting experience within a warm, welcoming, and quirky setting. The rustic interior features mismatched tables and chairs. Guests can taste wines on a sunny terrace overlooking the vines or in the cozy interior during cooler months. The vineyard's wine dog, Nellie, may greet visitors. Well-behaved pets are welcome, though they may be seated outside. A distinctive feature is the on-site Domescapes Glamping accommodation. Swinging Gate also participates in the Tamar Valley Mixed Dozen freight scheme, allowing visitors to collect wines from various vineyards and have them shipped home from the last stop.

## Food & Hospitality
Swinging Gate Vineyard does not operate a full restaurant on-site. However, they offer several food options for visitors, often outsourcing to local restaurants and chefs. Snacks available in the cellar door fridge include a range of Southern Sky Cheeses and crackers. During summer weekends, a pizza oven is sometimes fired up with a visiting chef, Nick, who is scheduled to be present every Saturday until March in 2025/26.

The vineyard hosts degustation wine dinners on the last Friday of every month, featuring local seasonal produce in collaboration with a local chef; booking ahead is recommended for these events. Occasional seasonal dinners and pop-ups are also held in conjunction with local chefs and restaurants. Additionally, the vineyard offers Domescapes Glamping accommodation.

## Awards & Recognition
No reliable information regarding specific awards, critic scores, or Halliday ratings for Swinging Gate Vineyard was found from public sources.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Swinging Gate Vineyard tends its vineyard in a holistic and sustainable manner. Their winemaking approach involves minimal intervention, and the fruit is hand-picked, crushed, and fermented using traditional methods. The winery has a focus on sustainability and a commitment to quality. Swinging Gate Vineyard holds Sustainable Tourism Accreditation by ATIC. They also produce "Sustainably Produced Wine".

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Swinging Gate Vineyard is 103 Glendale Road, Sidmouth, TAS 7270, Australia. It is located approximately a 30-minute scenic drive north of Launceston, or about 35-40 minutes from the heart of Launceston and 12 minutes from Exeter. The vineyard is less than an hour's drive from Launceston airport. The contact phone number is 0429 980 346, and the email address is swinginggatewine@gmail.com. Their website is http://www.swinginggatewines.com.au/. Swinging Gate Vineyard maintains a presence on social media platforms including Facebook and Instagram. The vineyard offers disabled access, and visitors are advised to contact the operator for details. Well-behaved pets are welcome at the cellar door, though they may be seated outside. The vineyard participates in the Tamar Valley Mixed Dozen freight scheme.

## Notable / Distinctive
Swinging Gate Vineyard is distinctive for having reopened in 2014 after a 15-year period of abandonment, with its name referencing the "gates swinging again". It is a family-run winery, notably featuring the wine dog Nellie. Winemaker Doug Cox, a former horticulturalist, is self-taught in winemaking. The vineyard benefits from old vines, originally planted in 1985. A unique offering is the Domescapes Glamping accommodation, featuring geodesic domes among the vines, some of which include outdoor bathtubs. The cellar door itself is housed in a converted old machinery shed. The winery is recognized for producing unusual varietals, blends, and methods, and is noted for having the only Frontenac, sparkling Sauvignon Blanc, and Amarone grown in Tasmania. It has been described as one of the "youngest wineries with one of the older vineyards in the Tamar".

